>h2 Guarded Object Publication
>tree open Guarded Object Publication
CupidBuild now has a hosted transaction for CupidASM ELF32 relocatable
objects. It freezes the source and checked seed, validates a private ET_REL
candidate through the shared ELF32 reader, runs strict CupidDis inspection,
rechecks the live boundaries, and publishes through a pinned parent. The
transaction records its acquired owner lock, checks it at both final
boundaries, and quarantines the path before cleanup. A successor lock is
restored instead of deleted.
The seed directory must contain exactly the five listed `.elf` or `.exe`
peers. All five frozen images must match the selected static ELF32 or strict
PE32 execution profile, and membership is checked again after both tools run.
The initial Linux and Windows standalone builds passed the real ISR object
path. The lock follow-up passes native host behavior and the checked Linux
object contract; fresh checked standalone execution remains open. The normal
build still uses the five-tool seed and Python publisher.
>endtree

>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
The CupidC, CupidLD, and CupidDis ELF path is separate from the CupidC source JIT.
Linux runs the checked i386 Linux seed directly. On Windows, checked native
CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD build and run the user ABI contract as a private
PE. Checked native CupidC, CupidLD, and CupidDis perform the output-bearing
compile, link, and inspection. An optional native Windows check
requires host-built CupidC and CupidLD output to match the checked seed.

kernel/lang/exec.cc currently supports:
- ELF32 executables
- i386 machine type
- ET_EXEC
- PT_LOAD segment loading

ELF programs are started as external processes and receive the syscall table
pointer as the first argument to _start.
>endtree

>h2 Checked-Seed Windows Image Proof
>tree open Checked-Seed Windows Image Proof
Checked-seed CupidLD also serializes one fixed-layout i386 PE32 console image.
It can build canonical import descriptors, lookup tables, IAT cells, and names
in writable .idata. Imported slots accept only known, zero-addend absolute
relocations. CupidLD orders imports with an in-place heap and keeps name
imports below the PE32 high-bit boundary. The fixed image has no
base-relocation table. It reserves and commits a one MiB stack; its heap
reserves one MiB and commits 4 KiB. The independent validator checks those
fields, reconstructs the exact .idata cursor, and rejects equivalent but
noncanonical layouts.

>h3 Source-Head PE Inspection
Source-head hosted CupidDis reads this deterministic static i386 PE32 profile.
It reports the bounded PE and COFF fields, canonical sections, and named
imports. Strict inspection decodes every executable section through the
shared x86 model and checks the entry point and constant direct local targets
against instruction starts. All five checked Windows tool images and an
import-free CupidLD fixture pass beside the independent Python validator.

Dynamic PE, base relocations, ordinal imports, PE symbols, and general section
layouts remain unsupported. The same applies to a loaded span above CupidLD's
2 GiB RVA limit. Complete section and import reports for the five checked tools
match an independent parse of their PE bytes. The promoted CupidDis tools
predate this reader, so a later fixed-point proof and seed promotion must
establish carriage.

CupidASM assembles the repository Windows entry, and freestanding CupidC
compiles its headerless main. CupidLD links GetStdHandle, WriteFile, and
ExitProcess imports. Windows validates and runs the image, requires the exact
stdout marker, empty stderr, and exit 37. The bootstrap report retains the
observed result and both stages' object and image hashes. At that checkpoint,
the promoted Linux seed carried this path. Its producers were static i386
Linux programs that ran through WSL on Windows. This proved the loader, not a
native Windows fixed point.

Each native bootstrap generation builds matching CupidASM, CupidC, CupidDis,
CupidLD, and CupidObj commands with Cupid tooling. Their shared hosted runtime
provides native arguments, allocation, separate standard streams, named-file
reads and writes, append behavior, seeking, the current directory, and useful
error mapping. A direct runtime contract checks those paths, including failed
opens and Windows quote and backslash rules. Each tool runs help plus a useful
success and failure path. CupidDis also checks exact raw-disassembly parity.
CupidLD adds path resolution, exclusive candidate creation, flush, replacement,
and cleanup. Its successful output matches the reference image, and its forced
failure leaves no candidate.

Those five PE32 images form the checked Windows execution seed used by
output-bearing production recipes. Fixed-point reconstruction, Toolchain
contracts still run the Linux seed through WSL. Artifact-size policy reads the
Linux manifest as provenance, while its checked contract runs as a temporary
PE on Windows. CUPSIZE2 also gives that contract the Windows manifest, Linux
parent digest, and five regular-file size and digest observations. The
contract validates the Windows target, provenance, inventory, and observed
bytes. The user ABI gate also builds and runs a temporary PE from the
Windows seed. The
native driver freezes the PE32 execution seed with a
separate verified Linux plan. The seed builds stage two, stage two builds stage
three, and stage three builds stage four. Stages two and three are transition
generations; the convergence check compares stages three and four. The former
stage-two to stage-three comparison stopped safely at cupidobj_main after
821.9 seconds on Windows and 883.3 seconds on Linux. New stack-probe code
generation changed compiler-produced objects. Later uncapped proofs passed.
Windows matched 20 C objects, two assembly objects, and five tools in 20
minutes 43 seconds with 5/5/5 behavior cases. Linux matched 19 C objects,
startup, and five tools in 24 minutes 22 seconds with 5/18/16 behavior cases.
Both reports bind the same 50-input snapshot, SHA-256
d8481a39e0d1c7f42779a8c9f5fc5de10d7e5b9bc4df63ce6afe9ddd9c9716da.
They began from uncommitted source and remain preliminary history. Linux later
passed a clean proof from revision
5d690c7508cc031a0cb32b2963bf16300b32e267 in 1,383.775 seconds. Stages three
and four matched 19 C objects, startup, and five tools, and both passed the
5/18/16 behavior matrix. The resulting 5,573-byte stage-four seed manifest has
SHA-256
f8528f5fcb68473f5078427dfc1c7dd5fce78413a56b45c6aa831971d827ca4f.
It binds the clean revision, generation four, the 50-input snapshot, and the
stage-three producers. A 1,411.998-second reproof reproduced all five seed
images and the same fixed point. Native Windows then passed a clean proof from
revision bd8fd28e6e0e097c4ee3a5c5de0b0706b7153930 in 1,152.7 seconds. Stages
three and four matched 20 C objects, two assembly objects, and five tools, and
both passed 5/5/5 behavior. The old seed matched stage two for CupidLD and
CupidObj but not for CupidASM, CupidC, or CupidDis. The 2,118-byte promoted
manifest has SHA-256
96bb80521ba679161008c9fa0891aff9d7ae172868cde107ff1a78feebdccfc9.
It keeps Linux manifest
f8528f5fcb68473f5078427dfc1c7dd5fce78413a56b45c6aa831971d827ca4f
as its verified parent. A 1,130.9-second reproof matched all five initial seed
images and repeated the fixed point. ADR 0268 records the shared runtime, ADR 0269
records CupidLD publication, ADR 0272 records Windows execution seed carriage
and production selection, ADR 0278 records the native driver, and ADR 0279
records the convergence rule. ADR 0280 records the clean Linux promotion, and
ADR 0281 records the clean Windows promotion.

A preliminary Linux behavior reconstruction matched four native tools but
found that its 387,584-byte CupidDis differed from the direct Windows image. The reconstructed
plan had omitted _WIN32=1 from cupiddis_main.cc. The corrected Windows profile,
parity test, and audit guard now cover all five tool mains.

The reproducible native operator runs make verify-windows-bootstrap-seed, then
make bootstrap-windows-from-seed. The second target publishes only under
build/bootstrap/checked-windows-seed after the complete gate passes. Its dry
run and two Make contract tests pass. The clean native Windows proof and
promotion are complete under ADR 0281.
>endtree

>h2 Entry Point
>tree open Entry Point
Use the wrapper header:
  user/cupid.h

Canonical entry:
  void _start(cupid_syscall_table_t *sys) {
      cupid_init(sys);
      ...
      exit();
  }

cupid_init(sys) stores the syscall table so wrapper functions can forward to
the kernel.
>endtree

>h2 Loader Constraints
>tree open Loader Constraints
kernel/lang/exec.cc applies these loader checks:
- ELF32 only
- little-endian
- executable type required
- i386 only
- at least one PT_LOAD segment (max ELF_MAX_PHDRS = 16)
- ordinary external ELF images must fit the exclusive 0x01C00000-0x01E00000
  arena, so their maximum span is 2 MB
- CupidC AOT images remain accepted in 0x01100000-0x01A00000
- CupidASM AOT images remain accepted in 0x01A00000-0x01C00000
- the complete PT_LOAD range must stay inside one accepted arena
- the entry point must be inside file-backed bytes of a PF_X PT_LOAD segment

If the binary violates those rules, exec rejects it.
>endtree

>h2 Build Flow
>tree open Build Flow
The user-space examples live under:
- user/examples/hello.cc
- user/examples/ls.cc
- user/examples/cat.cc

Build them with:
  make -C user

The Makefile names all as its default goal on Windows and Linux. Optional
native-driver oracle targets run only when requested by name.

The current user/Makefile writes outputs to:
  user/build/

The build freezes its CupidC, CupidLD, and CupidDis tool images, validates each
object and executable, and publishes only complete files. CupidDis requires
known instructions, valid local targets, and valid static code anchors in each
private ELF before publication. Linux runs the checked seed
directly. On Windows, checked native CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD build and
run the ABI contract. Checked native CupidC, CupidLD, and CupidDis then build
and inspect all six program artifacts directly.
Use `make test-user-cupidc-frontier` to repeat the deterministic build check.
On Windows, `make test-user-native-windows-equivalence` builds private
host-built driver snapshots and compares all six files with checked-seed
output.

Run `make sync-user` to build the image and stage the examples at FAT root.
A fresh image imports them into /home on first boot. Once HOMEFS.SYS exists,
later host-staged FAT files appear under /disk and must be run there or copied
into /home from the guest.
>endtree

>h2 Running ELF Programs
>tree open Running ELF Programs
Run a compiled binary by path:
  exec /home/hello

You can also disassemble instead of run:
  exec -d /home/hello

The shell prints the started PID when loading succeeds.
>button ls /home | shell:ls /home
>button ls /home/bin | shell:ls /home/bin
>endtree

>h2 Syscall Wrapper Surface
>tree open Syscall Wrapper Surface
user/cupid.h exposes wrappers around the syscall table for:
- console I/O
- memory allocation
- string helpers
- VFS file and directory calls
- process helpers
- shell execution
- uptime/time queries
- program execution

These wrappers expose the syscall table directly. CupidC instead uses
pre-registered bindings.

The current external-program table ABI is version 5. It contains 103
four-byte fields and occupies 412 bytes on i386. The version and table size
come first, followed by 101 kernel function pointers.

The public i386 scalar widths are:
- uint8_t: 1 byte, unsigned
- uint16_t: 2 bytes, unsigned
- uint32_t: 4 bytes, unsigned
- int32_t: 4 bytes, signed
- size_t: 4 bytes, unsigned

VFS names may use 128 bytes and paths may use 512 bytes. cupid_dirent_t is
136 bytes, with name at byte 0, size at byte 128, and type at byte 132.
cupid_stat_t is 8 bytes, with size at byte 0 and type at byte 4.

Before compiling a tracked example, the build runs the Cupid-built ABI
contract. Linux verifies or rebuilds the published static cohort and runs its
ELF contract. Windows freezes a 26-file closure, builds a private PE with
checked CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD, validates it, and runs it directly.
Either contract captures and rereads the same six kernel and public
declarations and checks the reviewed table, scalar, constant, record, and
provider rules. Python compares the report with an independent oracle. The
Windows path rechecks source and seed drift and leaves the Linux publication
untouched.
>endtree

>h2 Process Model
>tree open Process Model
ELF programs are created through process_create_with_arg_image_ex(...) with:
  PROCESS_DOMAIN_EXTERNAL

Image ownership is transferred into the process before its PID is published.
Ordinary external programs hold one generation-tagged exclusive arena lease;
cleanup releases the lease but never frees the permanently reserved pages.

The process table classifies ELF programs separately, but all programs still
run in one shared ring-0 address space.
>button ps | shell:ps
>endtree

>h2 Checked Build Reference
>tree open Checked Build Reference
The production path accepts the three tracked example sources and their exact
header closure. It uses the USER_I386 profile, then links with entry symbol
_start at the fixed external base. CupidDis applies --require-known,
--require-local-targets, and --require-code-anchors to the private executable.
Failure or output on either stream preserves the earlier file. Source, header,
tool-image, or control-file drift also stops publication. ADR 0326 records this
gate.

The Toolchain publication manifest includes a Cupid-built author stage. Python
freezes the publication facts, then frames a CUPMAN4 request with raw
stage-three and stage-four bytes for 58 pairs: 17 contract objects, 16 contract
executables, 19 bootstrap C objects, one startup object, and five tool images.
Stage-four checked Linux CupidC compiles the author, CupidASM builds its host
startup, and CupidLD links a static ELF on Linux or a validated PE on Windows.
The native PE uses the checked Windows runtime and exact imports. The author
requires regular, nonempty, byte-identical streams, hashes both sides, derives the 17 schema-v3
object records, and checks executable pairs against their artifact facts. It
derives the fixed-point summary from the exact pair inventories, so the request
has no caller all_equal field. Python repeats all 58 comparisons after author
acceptance and still owns filesystem traversal, drift checks, rollback, and
atomic publication. It captures each side from a regular file through a
descriptor whose identity is checked before and after the read. CUPMAN2
remains the publication verifier.

A valid schema-v3 publication binds exactly 21 artifacts, 75 publication
inputs, 55 bootstrap inputs, and 17 object comparisons, and CUPMAN2 must accept
the published cohort. Focused evidence passes 40 direct contract tests in
43.226 seconds, 62 publisher tests in 7.266 seconds, and 25 pinned runner tests
in 32.773 seconds. Both checked Python launchers resolve `tools` from this
checkout. The host bootstrap log records the exact publication
identity and completion evidence.

The publication gate requires stage-three and stage-four identity, hosted
runtime checks, live-input checks, and native Windows verification. The normal
`all` path uses Cupid tooling with the host code-generation variables poisoned.
Its exact policy covers fourteen paths: four OS artifacts, five Linux seed
images, and five Windows seed images. CUPSIZE2 gives the CupidC-built contract
the Windows manifest, Linux parent digest, and five regular-file size and
digest observations. The contract validates the Windows target, provenance,
exact inventory, and observed bytes. The focused semantic-contract,
checked-runner, and independent-policy modules contain 22, 16, and 13 tests,
for 51 total. They pass with four existing platform-specific skips. The host
bootstrap log records the complete source-consistent normal build, final
checked publication, and private four-vCPU proof for this increment. Exact OS
identities and runtime measurements stay there because this manual is an image
input.

Build and check all three examples:
  make test-user-cupidc-frontier

Compare native Windows output with the checked seed:
  make test-user-native-windows-equivalence

The local user/build directory is generated and ignored by Git. Rebuild the
programs before staging an image.

Stage them into the active image:
  make sync-user

Run the complete guest check:
  make test-user-cupidc-runtime

The 0x01C00000 base follows CupidASM's fixed arena. The kernel stack and the
CupidC and CupidASM ranges remain below it. The loader leases the external
arena exclusively because fixed-address programs cannot overlap.

Programs linked at an earlier fixed base must be rebuilt.

The runtime gate boots and executes hello, ls, and cat separately. It checks
hello's greeting and numeric output and confirms that ls reads the shell's
root directory. Every run starts from a private copy of the same staged
image. The cat copy also receives the fixed /disk/catfix.txt fixture at
/home/readme.txt. Cat keeps its normal HomeFS path, the selected image stays
unchanged, and the fixture supplies text shaped like a serial marker.
External print calls record only their PID, byte count, and FNV-1a
fingerprint, so file contents cannot forge a passing event. Every run must
finish with an exit marker and process exit for the PID that the loader
assigned.

A separate lease gate starts the same external program twice at 0x01C00000.
The first process exits and releases the arena before the second process
claims it and loads at the same address.

Private CupidC's data-free AOT output uses e_phnum=1 and one executable
PT_LOAD. Code remains at file offset 0x80. A data-bearing output keeps two
program headers, one for executable code and one for writable data.

Private CupidC tests also compile and execute callback calls in both JIT and
AOT modes. They cover named callback declarators and a file-scope,
signature-bearing function-pointer typedef on free-function parameters, Cupid
class method parameters, and declaration-initialized automatic objects,
including a float4 result in XMM0. A file object declared directly with the
typedef keeps the same signature through null or compatible function
initialization, checked plain assignment, indirect call, and clearing. A
defined function address is written into initialized data. A later definition
receives an absolute data patch before JIT or fixed-address AOT execution.

A named raw callback file object and direct free-function parameter retain the
same parsed signature without a typedef. The file object uses the same data
write or patch. The parameter uses the same cdecl slot and arity checks. The
private pool accepts 32 distinct raw signatures and recovers after rejecting
the next one. The private callback ABI module passes all 268 tests in 51.685
seconds. ADR 0315 records this boundary.

Reviewed native kernel bindings use the same signature representation in JIT
and fixed-address AOT output. Exact parameter metadata drives scalar and
floating conversion, complete cdecl slot layout, cleanup, arity, variadic
promotion, and result transport. A mixed int, double, float, int test preserves
all four slots in both modes. Unreviewed bindings remain on the named legacy
result-only path, so this change does not alter the external ELF loader ABI.
ADR 0332 records the boundary.

The active set_icon_drawer binding retains void(int, int) in the existing
recursive signature graph and publishes that child handle in its outer native
descriptor. Fixed-address AOT calls reject nested result, parameter,
record-identity, prototype, and variadic mismatches before jumping to the
kernel address. Corrupt handles, excessive depth, and capacity failures leave
the compiler state reusable. The 33-record backing pool preserves the existing
32-record source budget after the built-in descriptor is retained. ADR 0333
records this boundary.

The four-vCPU e1000 feature14 guest contract requires direct-call,
named-callback, typedef-parameter, typedef-global, and automatic-callback
evidence in that order. The retained paths must print
`[feature14-callback-typedef] PASS float4=4 calls=1`,
`[feature14-callback-global] PASS float4=4 initialized=1 assigned=1`
`cleared=1 calls=2`, and
`[feature14-callback-automatic] PASS local=4 method=4 calls=2`, followed by the
overall feature14 PASS and CupidC JIT completion. This private compiler boundary
does not change the external ELF loader ABI or the fixed process arena.
>endtree

>h2 When To Use ELF Instead Of CupidC
>tree open When To Use ELF Instead Of CupidC
Choose ELF when you want:
- a checked, reproducible external build
- direct syscall-table style programming
- fixed binary artifacts on disk
- loader/disassembly testing through exec and exec -d

Choose CupidC when you want:
- zero-build source execution
- REPL integration
- TempleOS-style source-first workflow
>endtree

>h2 Checked toolchain seed boundary
>tree
The promoted Linux and Windows cohorts are complete five-tool trust units.
Their fixed-point gates compare the final stages and exercise each platform's
success and failure behavior. CupidDis must reject unmatched executable
relocations.

Both cohorts carry the raw local-target policy used by the guarded bootloader
and SMP transactions. Exact seed identities and dated proof evidence are kept
in the host bootstrap log.

CupidDis also checks unrelocated direct targets inside each executable section
of a static ELF32 relocatable object. Relocated operand fields stay under the
separate relocation-ownership rule. Targets outside their source section or
inside an instruction fail. For static linked ET_EXEC input, direct targets may
cross executable load regions but must land on file-backed instruction starts.
The promoted cohorts carry both forms. Production object publication selects
the relocatable rule before replacement, and kernel publication selects the
linked rule for the pass-one and final ELFs before flattening.

Checked CupidDis has a static executable code-anchor rule. With
--require-known and --require-code-anchors, it checks e_entry and every defined
STT_FUNC symbol against decoded instruction starts in file-backed executable
code. Function aliases count separately. Undefined and absolute functions and
other symbol types do not count. Both promoted seeds carry this option. The
normal kernel publisher combines it with linked local-target validation for
the pass-one and final ELFs before flattening.
>endtree

